  <div class="col">
    <div class="eyebrow">The demo</div>
    <h2>An API gateway with six jobs</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="col">
    <p>Everyone in the room knows what a gateway does, and each of these jobs either keeps Ruby busy or needs state that every worker can see.</p>
    <ul class="list">
      <li><strong>Route by prefix</strong> to pools of upstream servers: <code>/api</code> to ports 9001 and 9002, <code>/static</code> and everything else to 9003 and 9004.</li>
      <li><strong>Balance by least outstanding requests</strong>, counted across every worker.</li>
      <li><strong>Rate limit per API key</strong>, with one count per key per second shared by every worker.</li>
      <li><strong>Cache</strong> <code>/static</code> responses for a second, in one cache shared by every worker.</li>
      <li><strong>Rewrite JSON</strong>: keep the fields the client asks for, camelCase every key, mask email addresses.</li>
      <li><strong>Report metrics</strong> for the whole process at <code>/metrics</code>, in Prometheus format.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>All of it is plain Ruby objects. Requests and responses are <code>Data</code> values, every feature is a layer with <code>call(request) { ... }</code> in the spirit of Rack, and the layers are frozen. That makes the whole routes table shareable with one magic comment, rate-limit windows, cache entries and metric counters included, and every Ractor gets the same table. Only sockets and upstream pools belong to a single Ractor.</p>
  </div>

