  </div>
</section>
<section id="sharing">
  <div class="col">
    <div class="eyebrow">The point of Ractors</div>
    <h2>Shared state without IPC</h2>
    <p>Forked workers share nothing they can change. A Puma cluster that wants one rate limit across workers needs Redis, metrics need a sidecar or memory-mapped files, and every worker keeps its own cache. Ractors live in one address space. Ruby lets them share frozen objects freely, and ratomic adds a few mutable structures that are safe to share: atomic counters, a concurrent map, a queue. So the gateway keeps its rate-limit windows, its cache and its metrics in one place, and all four Ractors update them in parallel.</p>
    <p>Each check below ran against four workers. Ractors, threads and Go each share one copy of the state; forks get four copies that drift apart.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="wide proof">
    <figure id="fig-metrics">
      <h3>One <code>/metrics</code> scrape, four workers</h3>
      <p class="cap">Share of all requests served that a single scrape reports, eight scrapes each.</p>
      <div class="tools"><div class="legend"></div><button class="tbl" type="button">Table</button></div>
      <div class="hbars"></div>
    </figure>
    <figure id="fig-limit">
      <h3>Rate limit of 1,000 per second</h3>
      <p class="cap">Requests let through in 10 seconds at an offered 5,000 per second. A 10-second run spans 11 windows, so the target is 11,000.</p>
      <div class="tools"><div class="legend"></div><button class="tbl" type="button">Table</button></div>
      <div class="hbars"></div>
    </figure>
    <figure id="fig-cache">
      <h3>Cache of 1,000 URLs, 1 second TTL</h3>
      <p class="cap">Requests that reached the upstream in 10 seconds at 40,000 per second. One shared cache needs 1,000 per second, 10,000 in all.</p>
      <div class="tools"><div class="legend"></div><button class="tbl" type="button">Table</button></div>
      <div class="hbars"></div>
    </figure>
  </div>
  <div class="col">
    <p>Forks fail all three in the expected way. Each scrape answers from whichever process caught the connection, so it reports a quarter of the traffic and a different number every time. Each process enforces the limit on its own, so <%= num(limit["ruby-fork"]) %> requests got through where <%= num(limit["ruby-ractor"]) %> did with Ractors. Each process has its own cache, so the backend saw <%= num(fetches["ruby-fork"]) %> requests instead of <%= num(fetches["ruby-ractor"]) %>. Threads get the sharing right but share one GVL: in the four seconds of the metrics check they served <%= num(share["ruby-thread"][:metrics][:served]) %> requests against <%= num(share["ruby-ractor"][:metrics][:served]) %> for Ractors.</p>
    <p>Two details are worth knowing before you copy this. Ractors let <%= limit["ruby-ractor"] - 11_000 %> more than the limit through, where threads and Go hit 11,000 exactly. The rate limiter creates each second's counter with <code>windows[key] ||= Counter.new</code>, and when two Ractors race to create it, one counter overwrites the other and loses a count or two. Go's <code>sync.Map#LoadOrStore</code> has no such race, and ratomic's <code>fetch_or_store</code> would not either, but it runs its block while holding a lock, which is exactly the pattern that deadlocked Ruby 4.0.6 in log entry 10. So the gateway uses only the map's <code>[]</code>, <code>[]=</code> and <code>delete</code>, and I put it through 15 seconds of eight Ractors storing and replacing shared entries while 30 global collections ran, without a crash.</p>
  </div>
</section>

